$8ED0 EndingLine7 ; "BANISHED."
@res stage5/textlines-ending.bin
$8EDD c DrawTextLine ; Draw one text line into a 96-byte page with the proportional TextFont
> Input: HL = the page (its low byte 0), IY = the line (header, characters, 0). Clears the 96 bytes from HL (12 bytes x 8 lines). The pen: DE = H, header byte 0; the pixel offset TextPenShift $FE4A = header byte 1; header byte 2 goes to TextScratch $FE4B (not read by the text code).
> Each character: '0'-'9' + $2C, space becomes $5B, then - 'A': A-Z are glyphs 0-25, space 26, `\` 27 (a full stop). The glyph is 8 bytes at TextFont + 8 x the index: byte 0 the width mask (kept in GlyphWidthMask $FE4C), 7 pixel rows. The rows go as pairs (row, 0) into the scratch $6A00-$6A0D (inside the play buffer), are shifted right by the pixel offset (TextPenShift $FE4A times SRL / RR over each pair), and ORed into the page at DE, DE + 1 (rows 12 bytes apart: pixel lines 0-6 of the line, line 7 stays blank). AdvanceTextPen moves the pen. A 0 ends the line (DrawTextLineDone) with IY past it, ready for the next line.
> Output: IY past the line's 0. Corrupts A, BC, DE, HL; TextPenShift $FE4A, TextScratch $FE4B, GlyphWidthMask $FE4C, $6A00-$6A0D.
> Witness: L1 42 (ending, rzx-r5, s5-right).
$8F04 DrawTextChar ; the next character; 0 ends the line
$8F16 DrawTextDigit ; '0'-'9' + $2C (no text uses a digit)
$8F1A DrawTextNotDigit ; space becomes $5B, glyph 26
$8F20 DrawTextGlyph ; index = A - 'A'; the glyph's width mask to $FE4C
$8F3A DrawTextCopyRow ; 7 rows into $6A00 as (row, 0) pairs
$8F4E DrawTextShift ; shift the 7 pairs right by the pixel offset (C times)
$8F53 DrawTextShiftRow
$8F5E DrawTextMerge ; OR the 7 pairs into the page, 12 bytes a row
$8F63 DrawTextMergeRow
$8F78 c AdvanceTextPen ; Move the pen past the glyph just drawn: its width + 1 pixel
> The width mask GlyphWidthMask $FE4C has the glyph's columns set from bit 7 down: C = 9 - its trailing zero bits (8 for most letters, 7 for C, E, F ..., 9 for M and W, 6 for space, 5 for I and the full stop). The pixel offset TextPenShift $FE4A + C; at 8 or more, DE + 1 and the offset - 8 (once: after a 9-wide glyph from offset 7 the offset stays 8, which the shift loop handles). Called after each glyph by DrawTextLine ($8F73).
> Witness: L1 456 (ending, rzx-r5, s5-right).
$8F7D AdvanceTextPenWidth ; count the mask's trailing zeros
$8F86 AdvanceTextPenAdd
$8F91 AdvanceTextPenStore
$8F95 c DrawTextLineDone ; The RET DrawTextLine jumps to at a line's 0
> Reached by JP Z from $8F0B.
> Witness: L1 42 (ending, rzx-r5, s5-right).
$8F96 b TextFont 8 ; The texts' proportional font: 28 glyphs of 8 bytes, A-Z, space, full stop
> Each glyph: byte 0 the width mask (bits set from bit 7 for the glyph's pixel columns plus a gap: $FE for A, B, D, N, O, Q, R, Y, Z; $FC for C, E, F, G, H, J, K, P, S, T, U, V, X; $F8 for L and space; $F0 for I and the full stop; $FF for M and W), then 7 rows of pixels (bit 7 the left). Read by DrawTextGlyph $8F20 (TextFont + 8 x the index). Indexes 28-36 (the digits) would read past it into UpdateBurst's code; no text has a digit.
$9066 TextFontSpace ; glyph 26: space (blank)
$906E TextFontStop ; glyph 27: the full stop, written `\` in the texts
@res stage5/bitmaps-text-font-1x8.bin

@module duel
@about When the hits on Dameron complete a phase, DuelHit $79A9 calls NextDameronForm: phases 1-3 throw seven staggered explosions (shot records of kind 4 at HudPicture $7000) round his head; phase 4 bursts the head's 16 x 12 shape at FinalBurstShape $7AD7 into particles (StartBurst), which fly apart for 14 steps of two passes (UpdateBurst from the main loop), each an 8 x 8 glyph drawn and erased directly on the screen. The particle list is at Particles $7100 (4 bytes a particle).
$9076 c UpdateBurst ; Move and draw the burst's particles one step (on the even passes)
> Called every pass by the main loop ($7869); acts only when PassCounter $FE4D is even. Nothing while BurstTimer $FE49 = $65 (set at $782E: no burst). Else BurstTimer + 1; at $1C ($0E when BurstSmall $FE48 is not 0) the burst ends (StopBurst). The step's glyph: BurstGlyphSteps[step + Random AND 3] x 8 + BurstGlyphs, written into ParticleGlyph's operand $915C.
> Each particle (4 bytes from Particles $7100, $FF ends the list, $80 a dead one): +2 the direction; 13-18 (the shape's right byte) become 0-5 and take the first set of steps, BurstSteps; 0-5 (the left byte) take the second, BurstStepsLeft. The step's index = BurstDirections[direction x 32 + step + (+3 AND 3)] (that sum doubled in the small mode); dx, dy = the pair at that index. The old cell is erased (EraseParticle, through TextPenShift $FE4A, TextScratch $FE4B); X + dx is kept when the sum is 1-30; Y - dy: a result of 0 leaves Y as it was, 26 and up kills the particle (+0 = $80, not drawn), else stored. Then the glyph's 8 bytes go to the screen cell (PlayLineTable[Y x 8] + X, INC H a line): a particle in rows 0-5 gets the address 0 + X (PlayLineTable's zero words) and writes into the ROM, i.e. is not shown.
> The even pass after StopBurst (BurstTimer $64) runs once more: $64 + 1 = $65 is not the end value, so the step $65 is drawn (the glyph and step indexes read past BurstGlyphSteps and BurstDirections), then the next even pass sees $65 and stops.
> In s5-right no burst ran: only the tests of PassCounter $FE4D and BurstTimer $FE49 ran. rzx-r5 and ending ran the body from $908D 16 times each, at the win.
> Witness: L1 11696 (ending, rzx-r5, s5-right).
$9087 UpdateBurstStep ; BurstTimer = $65: idle
$90BA UpdateBurstLoop ; the next particle: $FF ends, $80 skipped
$90D2 UpdateBurstDirection ; HL = BurstDirections + direction x 32
$90F2 UpdateBurstIndex
$9107 UpdateBurstMove ; E, D = dx, dy; erase the old cell
$912C UpdateBurstY
$913D UpdateBurstStoreY
$9140 UpdateBurstDraw ; the cell's screen address from PlayLineTable
$915B ParticleGlyph ; LD DE,n: n = the step's glyph, written at $90B3
$917F UpdateBurstNext
$918A b UnusedRet 1 ; A RET no code reaches
> After UpdateBurst's JP UpdateBurstLoop; no CALL, JP or word in stage 5's block names $918A (work/stage2/stage5/B-raw.py).
$918B c StopBurst ; BurstTimer = $64: the burst ends
> Reached by JP Z from $908F at the last step. As in stage 1 at $A393, the same code (stage 1's idle value; stage 5's idle value is $65, see UpdateBurst).
> Witness: L1 2 (ending, rzx-r5).
$9191 b BurstSteps 2 ; 20 (dx, dy) steps: 10 for the right-byte particles, 10 for the left-byte ones
> Signed bytes (-1, 0, 1): dx added to the column, dy taken from the character row. UpdateBurst reads the pair BurstSteps + 2 x the index ($90FA; + $14 for the directions 0-5). The same use as stage 1's BurstSteps $A39D.
$91A5 BurstStepsLeft ; the second set: the shape's left-byte particles (directions 0-5)
@res stage5/table-burst-steps.bin
$91B9 c StartBurst ; Turn a 16 x 12 shape into particles at $7100 that fly apart (UpdateBurst)
> Input: A = BurstSmall $FE48 (1 small: 14 steps of doubled moves; 0 big: 28), D, E = the shape's left column and top character row, IX = the shape (12 rows of 2 bytes, bit 7 the left). RandomPointer $FE0A = $0D7A (every burst flies the same way), BurstTimer $FE49 = $FF. Each set bit gives a particle of 4 bytes at Particles $7100: X, Y, the direction (a Random 0-5, + 13 for the row's right byte), +3 a Random 0-3; X + 1 a bit, the next row at Y + 1, X - 16; $FF ends the list. Preserves IY. Unlike stage 1's StartBurst $A1F3 it plays no sound and sets no colours.
> Its only use: NextDameronForm $A0E6 (JP) at phase 4, D = 12, E = 9, A = 1, IX = FinalBurstShape $7AD7 (Dameron's head).
> Witness: L1 2 (ending, rzx-r5).
$91CF StartBurstRow
$91D4 StartBurstByte
$91DD StartBurstBit
$91EB StartBurstDirection ; a Random 0-7 until under 6
$920C StartBurstNextBit
$9230 b BurstGlyphs 8 ; The burst's 8 x 8 particle glyphs: 0-3 from dense to sparse, 4-6 blank
> UpdateBurst writes BurstGlyphs + 8 x BurstGlyphSteps[...] into ParticleGlyph ($90AF); the indexes 0-3 are the four patterns, 4-6 (read only on the extra step $65, see UpdateBurst) are blank.
$9250 BurstGlyphBlank ; glyphs 4-6: zeros
@res stage5/bitmaps-particles-1x8.bin
$9268 b BurstUnused 4 ; Four zero bytes between the glyphs and BurstGlyphSteps
> No code reads them (UpdateBurst's glyph indexes stop at 6).
$926C b BurstGlyphSteps 16 ; The glyph (0-3) at each burst step: index = step + a Random 0-3
> 35 bytes: 1, 0 at the first steps, then 1, 2 and 3, 2: the particles grow sparser as they fly. Read by UpdateBurst ($90A2: step 0-$1B + 0-3, the first 31 bytes; the extra step $65 reads past the table).
@res stage5/table-burst-glyphs.bin
$928F b BurstDirections 32 ; Six direction tables of 32 step indexes (0-6 into BurstSteps); the first five here
> UpdateBurst reads BurstDirections + direction x 32 + (step + 0-3) ($90DA; the sum doubled in the small mode). The sixth table (direction 5, BurstDirectionsCut $932F) has only 26 bytes before EraseParticle's code at EraseParticle $9349: its indexes 26-31 read code bytes, and the small mode's doubled indexes up to 32 reach $934F.
@res stage5/table-burst-directions.bin
$932F b BurstDirectionsCut 26 ; The sixth direction table (direction 5), cut to 26 bytes by EraseParticle's code
> Its indexes 26-31 (and the small mode's doubled ones up to 32) would read the code bytes of EraseParticle $9349 on: UpdateBurst does not check the table's end. rzx-r5 and ending read 13 of its bytes and 4 bytes of EraseParticle $9349 on: the overrun happens.
@res stage5/table-burst-directions-cut.bin
$9349 c EraseParticle ; Clear the 8 x 8 screen cell of the particle at column $FE4A, row $FE4B
> HL = PlayLineTable[TextScratch $FE4B x 8] + TextPenShift $FE4A; 8 zero bytes, INC H a line. Called by UpdateBurst ($9119) before each particle moves. Corrupts A, BC, HL.
> Witness: L1 2754 (ending, rzx-r5).

@module render
@about Stage 5 draws the duel straight to the screen: the shots, explosions and the hero's other frames come from one sprite bank at SpriteBank $B3BA (SpriteFrameAddress), the screen lines from tables (the resident ScreenLineTable $FB00 and two copies in the block, StageScreenLines and PlayLineTable). Stage 1's play-buffer sprite routines (DrawSprite and the others) are still in the block with no caller.
$9378 c SpriteFrameAddress ; DE = $B3BA + SpriteFrameOffsets[A]: the bytes of sprite frame A
> Input: A = the frame 0-23. Output: DE = the frame's first byte. Preserves BC and HL. Callers: the straight shots $80B3 (frame PassCounter AND 3), the explosions $81ED (frames 19-23), $8383, DrawHeroFrame $9D8F (frames 12-23) and MoveCurvedShot $9E42 (frames 4-7).
> Witness: L1 13871 (ending, rzx-r5, s5-right).
$938E w SpriteFrameOffsets 8 ; The offset of each of the 24 sprite frames in the bank $B3BA
> 0, $30 ... $150 (frames 0-7, 48 bytes each: 3 bytes x 16 lines), $180, $19B, $1B6, $1D1 (frame 8 27 bytes after frame 7's 48; frames 8-11 27 bytes: 3 x 9), $1EC, $234 (frames 12-13, 72 bytes: 3 x 24), $27C (frame 14, 144 bytes to the next), $30C, $354 ... $54C (frames 15-23, 72 bytes apart). Read by SpriteFrameAddress ($937F).
@res stage5/table-frame-offsets.bin

@module util
@about Most of these recur byte for byte from stage 1 (tools/stage_names.py) and keep stage 1's names. Stage 1's actor records and missile list ($74E0, HeroShots $75E0) are not used by stage 5's duel: their routines (SpawnActor to UpdateMissiles) have no caller in stage 5's code.
$93BE c TestSpaceKey ; Is SPACE down?
> Calls KeyHook $FCDB, then reads port $7FFE bit 0: Z when SPACE is held. Called from $7BD4, ItemWindowSpace $7C02 and ItemWindowRelease $7C08. As in stage 1 at $8A54, the same code.
> Not executed in any recording (the recordings checked: s5-right, rzx-r5, ending); known only from the code.
$93C9 c ClearAttributes ; Clear the screen's 768 attributes to 0
> Called by ClearScreen. As in stage 1 at $8A5F, the same code.
> Witness: L1 3 (ending, rzx-r5, s5-right).
$93D7 c ClearShadowAttributes ; Clear the shadow attributes $6D00-$6FFF to 0
> Called by StageSetUp. As in stage 1 at $8A6D, the same code.
> Witness: L1 3 (ending, rzx-r5, s5-right).
$93E5 c ClearPlayBuffer ; Clear $5B00-$6C30 to 0 (LDIR)
> 4401 bytes. As in stage 1 at $8A7B, the same code. No caller in stage 5's code (no CALL, JP or word in the block names $93E5: work/stage2/stage5/B-raw.py).
> Not executed in any recording (the recordings checked: s5-right, rzx-r5, ending); known only from the code.
$93F3 c CopyScreenToBlock ; Copy a block of the screen, one pixel line at a time, into a buffer
> HL = the buffer (lines packed one after the other), B = the screen column, C = the first pixel line, D = the width in bytes, E = the lines. Patches its ADD A,n at CopyBlockColumn with the column; the line addresses come from ScreenLineTable $FB00. Each line: EX DE,HL, LDIR, EX DE,HL, so the screen's bytes go to the buffer (not the other way). Called from $7924 (EndStage saves the HUD's lines 0-47 into HudPicture $7000). The same code as stage 1's $8A89, which stage 1 names CopyBlockToScreen.
> Not executed in any recording (the recordings checked: s5-right, rzx-r5, ending); known only from the code.
$9403 CopyBlockLine
$9407 CopyBlockColumn ; ADD A,n: n = the column, written at $93F4

@module hud
@about The HUD (screen lines 0-47) is stage 1's: the picture $7000 dissolved in, the boxes coloured by FillShadowAttributes, the score written by NumberToDigits and ByteToDigits, the energy bar drawn by DrawEnergyBar from Energy $FE4F.
$941B c DissolveHud ; Dissolve the HUD's 48 pixel lines onto the screen in 8 steps
> DE = the source picture (48 lines of 32 bytes; HudPicture $7000 from StageSetUp). Eight steps, each after a HALT: the low byte of RandomPointer $FE0A = 0 (the same pattern every step), then every byte of screen lines 0-47 (ScreenLineTable $FB00) = source AND a mask; the mask is read at $F9CB + (a byte read through RandomPointer AND $18) + the step + 1 (the resident's DissolveMasks $F9CC: 4 rows of 8) and gains bits each step. Patches the LD DE at DissolveSource and the ADD A at DissolveStepOperand. As stage 1's DissolveHud $8AB1.
> Witness: L1 3 (ending, rzx-r5, s5-right).
$9426 DissolveStep
$9431 DissolveSource ; LD DE,n: n = the picture, written at $941B
$9436 DissolveLine
$9446 DissolveByte
$9455 DissolveStepOperand ; ADD A,n: n = the step 0-7

